The latest stable MSTest is **4.3.2**. Upgrading from 3.8.0 is a major version jump with several breaking changes. Here's what affects **your code specifically**:

---

## Breaking Changes Affecting Your Project

### 1. `[ExpectedException]` is removed ❌

Your two test methods use `[ExpectedException(...)]`, which no longer exists in v4. Replace with `Assert.ThrowsExactly<T>()`:

```csharp
// Before
[ExpectedException(typeof(ArgumentNullException))]
[TestMethod]
public void Validate_NullInput_ThrowsArgumentNull()
{
    Validate(null);
}

// After
[TestMethod]
public void Validate_NullInput_ThrowsArgumentNull()
{
    Assert.ThrowsExactly<ArgumentNullException>(() => Validate(null));
}
```

### 2. `Assert.IsTrue(true)` triggers warning MSTEST0032

In v4 this is **Warning** severity (was Info). It flags always-true assertions. Replace with a meaningful assertion or remove it.

---

## Other Breaking Changes (not in your code, but good to know)

| Change | Impact |
|--------|--------|
| `TestMethodAttribute.Execute` → `ExecuteAsync` | Only if you have custom test method attributes |
| `ClassCleanupBehavior` enum removed | Remove `ClassCleanupBehavior.EndOfClass` param |
| `TestContext.Properties` is now `IDictionary<string, object>` | Use `.ContainsKey()` not `.Contains()` |
| `TestTimeout` enum removed | Use `[Timeout(int.MaxValue)]` |
| `Assert.ThrowsException` removed | Use `Assert.ThrowsExactly` |
| Assert `message + params object[]` → just `message` | Use string interpolation |
| Min TFM is net8.0+ (you're fine) | .NET 7 and below dropped |
| Many analyzers promoted to Warning | More warnings on build |

---

## How to Fix Your Project

Update the package version in `.csproj`:

```xml
<PackageReference Include="MSTest" Version="4.3.2" />
```

Then update `ValidationServiceTests.cs`:

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void Validate_NullInput_ThrowsArgumentNull()
{
    Assert.ThrowsExactly<ArgumentNullException>(() => Validate(null));
}

[TestMethod]
public void Process_InvalidState_ThrowsInvalidOperation()
{
    Assert.ThrowsExactly<InvalidOperationException>(() => Process());
}
```

